default retry policy of httr2 does (two tries, D170). \code{decide()} sends
all such requests again together, after the longest wait that their
\code{Retry-After} headers ask for (1 second without the header, at most 60
seconds). A second failure stays as a row error. So a call never waits
long for a server that keeps refusing.

An interrupt (Esc) keeps the rows that are done. After an interrupt
while the requests are out, nothing is sent again, and each request
that did not finish gets a row error. An interrupt during the wait
before the second tries skips them; their rows get the error
"Interrupted before a retry." With \code{path}, an interrupt also stops the
chunks: the file keeps every finished answer, the inputs that were not
sent get the row error of a request that did not finish, and a new
call sends only the rest. Each write goes to a temporary file in the
same folder first, which then replaces the file, so a crash during a
write leaves the previous file whole. When the file cannot be replaced
(for example, while a sync program holds it), \code{decide()} tries again
twice, and warns at the end with the number of finished answers that
the file does not hold.

No row is lost. A row has \code{NA} answers and a message in \code{.error} when
the call fails (the message holds the error text of the provider word
for word, the model name and the size of the request in bytes), when
the response cannot be read, when an answer is not an allowed answer or
is missing, or when the probabilities of a score question are all zero
or incomplete. A row with an answered call keeps its \code{.input_tokens}
and \code{.cost}. A \code{model}, \code{input_tokens} or \code{cost} field that is not one
value gives the model of the decider or \code{NA}.

\code{decide()} reads the key and checks the input type of the model before
any request. For a setup with a calibrator, it also checks before any
request that probably and the packages of the calibrator (for example
mgcv) are installed. It prepares each reference image once per call (a
setup holds its references prepared) and each input image once. With
\code{decider_systemone(image_format = "images")}, it writes the images with
three color channels.

SystemOne links reference images to the answers of choice and noul
questions. A SystemOne score question has no place for them: they are
sent, but not linked to a level. OpenAI puts them in the instructions
of the question, and the image-list format names them in the state
text.

\code{.cost} holds only a cost that the provider reports (OpenRouter does).
When no row has a cost, \code{decide()} prints one message that says so.

\code{.seconds} is the total time of the response as
\code{httr2::resp_timing()} gives it; for a request that was sent again, it
is the time of the second try. It is \code{NA} when no response arrived.
